    "https://lsu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/LSU/job/0030-LSU-Library/Director-of-Collections-Services_R00118475": {
      "title": "Director of Collections Services",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-11\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nAll Job Postings will close at 12:01a.m. CST (1:01a.m. EST) on the specified Closing Date (if designated). If you close the browser or exit your application prior to submitting, the application progress will be saved as a draft. You will be able to access and complete the application through “My Draft Applications” located on your Candidate Home page. Job Posting Title: Director of Collections Services     Position Type: Faculty     Department: LSUAM Lib - PS - Collections & Open Scholarship (Hayley Johnson (00049583))     Work Location: 0030 LSU Library     Pay Grade: Academic     Job Description: Reporting to the Head of Collection Services and Open Scholarship the Director of Collections Services provides strategic leadership for LSU Libraries’ collections, advancing the Libraries’ mission to support the University’s teaching, research, and learning through innovative, sustainable, and user-centered collection strategies. This role integrates evolving collection models, budget planning, and assessment practices to align with the Libraries’ strategic priorities. They oversee collection development, assessment, and stewardship of physical, digital, and open-access resources, ensuring alignment with institutional priorities, fostering data-driven decision-making, and championing sustainable, user-centered models in library collections. They work closely with library units and academic departments to guide renewal, selection, and retention decisions, ensuring a sustainable and relevant collection. They explore innovative approaches to collection development, analyze financial and usage data, and refine policies to ensure fiscally responsible decision-making. Responsibilities: 40% Collection Strategy: Provide vision and direction for the development and stewardship of research collections. Collaborate with the Head of Collections & Open Scholarship, subject librarians, faculty, and campus stakeholders to ensure collections align with evolving institutional priorities. Develop and implement policies, procedures, and guidelines for lifecycle management of resources in all formats. Negotiate pricing and content with publishers and vendors, using data-driven assessments to maximize impact and cost-effectiveness. Manage vendor relationships to ensure quality service and strategic acquisitions. Coordinate with internal selectors and vendors on approval plans. Represent LSU Libraries in consortia discussions, system-wide committees, and national and regional networks to assess collaborative agreements and emerging best practices. 30% Data, Assessment & Budget Strategy: Leads assessment initiatives to measure the value, use, and impact of collections. Stays informed of best practices and emerging methodologies in collection assessment. In collaboration with the Head of Collections & Open Scholarship, oversees the development of a comprehensive data analysis strategy to support collection assessment and data-informed decision-making, including the creation of analytics, dashboards, and benchmarking tools to monitor usage, cost, return on investment, and trends across peer institutions. Collaborates with Administrative Services and the Associate Dean of Public & Collection Services on budget forecasting, allocation, reallocation, cost‐benefit analysis, and long-term sustainability of collection investments. Compile and submit required reports ensuring accuracy and timeliness. 20% Supervision & Staff Development: Supervise and mentor staff within the Collections and Resource Strategies department, setting performance goals and providing regular evaluations. Identify training and professional development needs to ensure staff maintain current knowledge of collection trends, tools, and practices. Encourage a culture of innovation, collaboration, and shared responsibility among the team. 10% Service: Performs service to the libraries, university, and/or profession. Minimum Qualifications: Master's, MLIS/MLS from an ALA-accredited institution. 5 years of supervisory or leadership experience. Progressively responsible experience in academic or research library collection development, acquisitions/licensing, metadata/cataloging, or resource management. Demonstrated experience in budget management, data‐driven decision making, and analytics in a library collections context. Experience with integrated library systems (ILS) or library services platforms (LSP), and familiarity with metadata/cataloging standards and discovery systems.     Additional Job Description: Special Instructions: \"Please provide a cover letter, resume, and (3) professional references. Attach pdf document(s) under the \"\"Resume/CV\"\" section of your application. A copy of your transcript(s) may be attached to your application. However, official transcripts are required prior to hire. For questions or concerns regarding the status of your application or salary ranges, please contact libadminsvs@lsu.edu \"     Skills Needed:     Preferred Skills:     Posting Date: August 11, 2026     Closing Date (Open Until Filled if No Date Specified): February 7, 2027    Additional Position Information: Background Check - An offer of employment is contingent on a satisfactory pre-employment background check. Benefits - LSU offers outstanding benefits to eligible employees and their dependents including health, life, dental, and vision insurance; flexible spending accounts; retirement options; various leave options; paid holidays; wellness benefits; tuition exemption for qualified positions; training and development opportunities; employee discounts; and more!     Positions approved to work outside the State of Louisiana shall be employed through Louisiana State University’s partner, nextSource Workforce Solutions, for Employer of Record Services including but not limited to employment, benefits, payroll, and tax compliance. Positions employed through Employer of Record Services will be offered benefits and retirement as applicable through their provider and will not be eligible for State of Louisiana benefits and retirement.     Essential Position (Y/N):     LSU is an Equal Opportunity Employer.     All candidates must have valid U.S. work authorization at the time of hire and maintain that valid work authorization throughout employment. Changes in laws, regulations, or government policies may impact the university’s ability to employ individuals in certain positions.     HCM Contact Information: For questions or concerns related to updating your application with attachments (e.g., resumes, RS:17 documents), date of birth, or reactivating applications, please contact the LSU Human Resources Management Office at 225-578-8200 or email HR@lsu.edu .  For questions or concerns regarding the status of your application or salary ranges, please contact the department using the information provided in the Special Instructions section of this job posting."
    },
    "https://lsu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/LSU/job/0030-LSU-Library/Acquisitions-Librarian_R00118319": {
      "title": "Acquisitions Librarian",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-07\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nAll Job Postings will close at 12:01a.m. CST (1:01a.m. EST) on the specified Closing Date (if designated). If you close the browser or exit your application prior to submitting, the application progress will be saved as a draft. You will be able to access and complete the application through “My Draft Applications” located on your Candidate Home page. Job Posting Title: Acquisitions Librarian     Position Type: Faculty     Department: LSUAM Lib - PS - Collection Management (Jacob Fontenot (00002518))     Work Location: 0030 LSU Library     Pay Grade: Academic     Job Description: The Acquisitions Librarian is responsible for all resource acquisitions operations for LSU Libraries. The Acquisitions Librarian will oversee efficient strategies and methodologies for obtaining and processing new print and electronic resources and content for both monographic and serial resources. This position will also foster partnerships with other library colleagues involved in collection development, budgeting, and resource discovery and access. The Acquisitions Librarian has overall responsibility for disbursement of the Libraries’ acquisitions budget, working closely with the Head of Collection Services on budget administration and analysis. Provides supervision and guidance to unit staff, including training and professional development support. The Acquisitions Librarian regularly evaluates work methods and workflows considering new services and technological advancements to strengthen operations and purchasing relationships through innovative acquisition strategies and solutions. The Acquisitions Librarian initiates and maintains relationships with booksellers, subscription agents, and content providers. Responsibilities: 60% Acquisitions: Coordinate the ordering, receiving, and invoicing of print and electronic resources, including monographs, serials, databases, and streaming media. Ensure compliance with LSU and state procurement policies and maintain accurate acquisitions records in the library management system. Collaborate with Administrative Services to monitor and reconcile fund balances, review expenditures, and track budget allocations. Serve as liaison with vendors, publishers, and subscription agents to facilitate purchasing and resolve billing issues. Responsible for the accuracy and integrity of the acquisitions management information system. Maintain and update acquisitions workflows, documentation, and statistics; identify areas for improvement and propose solutions. Resolves complex acquisitions problems and applies knowledge of current issues and trends in acquisitions, scholarly communications, and the publishing industry to improve services. In collaboration with the Director of Collection Services, monitors library materials expenditures. Develops/analyzes statistical and financial data and prepares reports for Director of Collection Services, subject librarians and the Library administration. 15% Assessment: Assists the Head of Collection Services in the collection, analysis, and presentation of acquisitions data for annual reporting, audits, and strategic collection planning. Support subject librarians by providing data and analysis on fund use, expenditure trends, and cost-per-use information. Stay informed of trends and best practices in acquisitions, scholarly publishing, and open access business models. 10% Supervision & Staff Development: Supervise and mentor staff within the Collections and Resource Strategies department, setting performance goals and providing regular evaluations. Identify training and professional development needs to ensure staff maintain current knowledge of collection trends, tools, and practices. 10% Performs service to the libraries, university, and/or profession. 5% Performs other duties, as assigned. Minimum Qualifications: Master’s degree in Library & Information Science from an ALA-accredited program. 3 years related working experience and 1 year of supervisory experience. Experience with acquisitions, electronic resources, serials, collection development, procurement, or finance in higher education or libraries. Experience with library services platforms such as SirsiDynix, Alma, GOBI, OCLC Connexion, or OCLC Worldshare. Preferred Qualification s: Knowledge of acquisitions functions including monographic ordering, approval plans, licensing, renewals for print and e-resources, invoicing, and accounting for library collection resources. Experience with library financial processes, vendor management, or university procurement systems. Knowledge of budget management, fund accounting, or fiscal reconciliation in a library context.     Additional Job Description: Special Instructions: \"Please provide a cover letter, resume, and (3) professional references. Attach pdf document(s) under the \"\"Resume/CV\"\" section of your application. A copy of your transcript(s) may be attached to your application. However, official transcripts are required prior to hire. For questions or concerns regarding the status of your application or salary ranges, please contact libadminsvs@lsu.edu \"     Skills Needed:     Preferred Skills:     Posting Date: August 7, 2026     Closing Date (Open Until Filled if No Date Specified): February 3, 2027    Additional Position Information: Background Check - An offer of employment is contingent on a satisfactory pre-employment background check. Benefits - LSU offers outstanding benefits to eligible employees and their dependents including health, life, dental, and vision insurance; flexible spending accounts; retirement options; various leave options; paid holidays; wellness benefits; tuition exemption for qualified positions; training and development opportunities; employee discounts; and more!     Positions approved to work outside the State of Louisiana shall be employed through Louisiana State University’s partner, nextSource Workforce Solutions, for Employer of Record Services including but not limited to employment, benefits, payroll, and tax compliance. Positions employed through Employer of Record Services will be offered benefits and retirement as applicable through their provider and will not be eligible for State of Louisiana benefits and retirement.     Essential Position (Y/N):     LSU is an Equal Opportunity Employer.     All candidates must have valid U.S. work authorization at the time of hire and maintain that valid work authorization throughout employment. Changes in laws, regulations, or government policies may impact the university’s ability to employ individuals in certain positions.     HCM Contact Information: For questions or concerns related to updating your application with attachments (e.g., resumes, RS:17 documents), date of birth, or reactivating applications, please contact the LSU Human Resources Management Office at 225-578-8200 or email HR@lsu.edu .  For questions or concerns regarding the status of your application or salary ranges, please contact the department using the information provided in the Special Instructions section of this job posting."
    },
    "https://lsu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/LSU/job/0102-Music-School-Building/Cordell-and-Ava-Haymon-Endowed-Chair-of-Music-Therapy---Associate-or-Full-Professor-of-Music_R00110758": {
      "title": "Cordell and Ava Haymon Endowed Chair of Music Therapy & Associate or Full Professor of Music",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-08-03\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nAll Job Postings will close at 12:01a.m. CST (1:01a.m. EST) on the specified Closing Date (if designated). If you close the browser or exit your application prior to submitting, the application progress will be saved as a draft. You will be able to access and complete the application through “My Draft Applications” located on your Candidate Home page. Job Posting Title: Cordell and Ava Haymon Endowed Chair of Music Therapy & Associate or Full Professor of Music     Position Type: Faculty     Department: LSUAM CMDA - School of Music (James L Byo (00010414))     Work Location: 0102 Music School Building     Pay Grade: Academic     Job Description: Louisiana State University invites applications for the position of the Cordell and Ava Haymon Endowed Chair of Music Therapy, an endowed, tenured faculty appointment at the rank of Associate Professor or Professor. The successful candidate will provide visionary leadership for LSU's rapidly growing Music Therapy program within the School of Music in the College of Music & Dramatic Arts (CMDA).This endowed position offers a rare opportunity to lead the first music therapy degree program established at a public university in Louisiana and to shape the future of music therapy education, research, and clinical practice across the state and the Gulf South. Established through the generosity of the Haymon family, whose multigenerational commitment has been instrumental in advancing music therapy at LSU, the endowed chair provides significant annual resources to support innovative research, instructional excellence, clinical partnerships, faculty and student development, and strategic program growth. LSU welcomed its inaugural Bachelor of Music Therapy cohort in Fall 2023. With a strong undergraduate curriculum now established, the program is poised for its next phase of growth, including expanded enrollment, transformative clinical partnerships, externally funded research, and the development of graduate education opportunities. Situated within a Carnegie R1 research university, the Chair will play a central role in defining a long-term vision for the program while expanding access to high-quality music therapy education and services in a region where such opportunities remain limited. The Chair reports to the Director of the School of Music and will collaborate extensively across LSU's expanding research and healthcare enterprise, including LSU Health New Orleans, LSU Health Shreveport, the LSU AgCenter, the Pennington Biomedical Research Center, and partners throughout the healthcare, education, and community sectors. These collaborations create exceptional opportunities to advance interdisciplinary research and clinical innovation in areas such as cancer care, behavioral health, rehabilitation, integrative medicine, survivorship, symptom management, and healthcare equity. Located in a state internationally recognized for its rich and diverse musical heritage, LSU provides a distinctive environment where music serves as both a defining cultural force and a powerful vehicle for health, healing, and community engagement. The successful candidate will have the opportunity to build a nationally recognized program that advances scholarship, transforms clinical practice, and strengthens the role of music therapy in improving health and well-being throughout Louisiana, the Gulf South, and beyond. Job Responsibilities: Provide visionary leadership for LSU's Music Therapy program, including strategic planning, program development, faculty leadership, accreditation, and oversight of clinical education and operations. Teach undergraduate and graduate courses in music therapy while mentoring students, supervising clinical training, and ensuring excellence in professional preparation. Lead a nationally recognized program of scholarship by advancing innovative, externally funded research and maintaining a distinguished national and international reputation in music therapy. Cultivate interdisciplinary partnerships across LSU's health, research, and clinical enterprise to expand music therapy research, clinical practice, and community engagement. Develop and sustain clinical affiliations with healthcare systems, community organizations, and regional partners to enhance experiential learning and expand access to music therapy services. Recruit, mentor, and support faculty and students in fostering a collaborative, inclusive, and high-achieving academic environment. Provide leadership in university governance and professional service , contributing to departmental, college, university, and national initiatives that advance the field of music therapy. Represent LSU and the Music Therapy program through professional organizations, public engagement, and partnerships that elevate the university's leadership in music therapy education, research, and clinical innovation. Minimum Qualifications: Earned doctorate (Ph.D., DMA, or equivalent terminal degree) in Music Therapy or a closely related discipline with a demonstrated focus on music therapy. Board Certification in Music Therapy (MT-BC) or equivalent profesional credential. Eligibility for appointment with tenure at the rank of Associate Professor or Professor. Demonstrated excellence in university-level teaching. Evidence of mastery of professional music therapy competencies and advanced clinical practice. Experience developing clinical partnerships in medical, educational, behavioral health or community settings. Record of scholarly achievement, including peer-reviewed publications and other evidence of sustained research activity. Demonstrated ability to mentor student scholarship. Preferred Qualifications: Establish national or international reputation in music therapy research, clinical practice, education or leadership. Experience working with a research-intensive university environment. Experience pursuing federal funding opportunities, including NIH or related agencies. Experience supervising music therapy clinical training programs and overseeing internship or practicum experiences. Record of interdisciplinary collaboration involving healthcare, psychology, education, neuroscience, rehabilitation, public health or related fields. Leadership in local, regional, national, or international professional organizations. Leadership in program development, enrollment growth, accreditation or strategic planning. LSU's College of Music & Dramatic Arts comprises two schools, Music and Theatre & Film. It is a performing arts community that trains, cultivates, prepares, and rewards excellent artists, educators, scholars, and performers, fostering passion for the arts, collaboration, and the creation of new works. We engage with our community, heightening our collective ability to communicate with expressive power and purpose. The LSU School of Music stands as one of the nation's most comprehensive music programs and serves as a premier cultural arts center for the state of Louisiana. Nestled within the College of Music & Dramatic Arts at Louisiana State University in Baton Rouge, the School has cultivated a rich tradition of musical excellence for nearly a century — earning its initial accreditation from the National Association of Schools of Music (NASM) in 1931, a distinction it has held and renewed ever since, with its most recent comprehensive review completed in 2024. With a vibrant community of nearly 500 music majors, students find themselves immersed in a tight-knit, artistically driven environment where world-class instruction meets Louisiana's uniquely rich cultural heritage. The School offers a remarkable breadth of degree options across every level of study — from undergraduate degrees in Music Performance, Music Education, Music Therapy, and Music Composition to graduate programs including the Master of Music, Doctor of Musical Arts, and PhD in Music. Students learn from an acclaimed faculty of renowned scholars, stellar performers, and inspiring teachers with active international careers, supplemented by a world-renowned network of visiting artists at the forefront of their fields. Graduate students in particular benefit from a hands-on, 21st-century curriculum that emphasizes flexibility, artistic expression, communication, and technology — preparing them not just to perform, but to discover, create, and lead in a rapidly evolving musical landscape.     Additional Job Description: Special Instructions: Review of applications begins on September 14, 2026. To receive full consideration, please submit a letter of application, curriculum vitae, contact information for three references, teaching philosophy (one page), and summary of research program with links to two most influential publications (one page). Teaching videos may be requested at a later date. Official transcripts are required prior to hire. For questions or concerns regarding the status of your application or salary ranges, please contact Joseph Dunbar at jdunbar@lsu.edu or Lauren Hensel at lfoster1@lsu.edu     Skills Needed:     Preferred Skills:     Posting Date: August 3, 2026     Closing Date (Open Until Filled if No Date Specified): January 30, 2027    Additional Position Information: Background Check - An offer of employment is contingent on a satisfactory pre-employment background check. Benefits - LSU offers outstanding benefits to eligible employees and their dependents including health, life, dental, and vision insurance; flexible spending accounts; retirement options; various leave options; paid holidays; wellness benefits; tuition exemption for qualified positions; training and development opportunities; employee discounts; and more!     Positions approved to work outside the State of Louisiana shall be employed through Louisiana State University’s partner, nextSource Workforce Solutions, for Employer of Record Services including but not limited to employment, benefits, payroll, and tax compliance. Positions employed through Employer of Record Services will be offered benefits and retirement as applicable through their provider and will not be eligible for State of Louisiana benefits and retirement.     Essential Position (Y/N):     LSU is an Equal Opportunity Employer.     All candidates must have valid U.S. work authorization at the time of hire and maintain that valid work authorization throughout employment. Changes in laws, regulations, or government policies may impact the university’s ability to employ individuals in certain positions.     HCM Contact Information: For questions or concerns related to updating your application with attachments (e.g., resumes, RS:17 documents), date of birth, or reactivating applications, please contact the LSU Human Resources Management Office at 225-578-8200 or email HR@lsu.edu .  For questions or concerns regarding the status of your application or salary ranges, please contact the department using the information provided in the Special Instructions section of this job posting."
    },
    "https://lsu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/LSU/job/Veterinary-Medicine/Assistant-Professor-of-Anatomy_R00118014": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Anatomy",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-31\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nAll Job Postings will close at 12:01a.m. CST (1:01a.m. EST) on the specified Closing Date (if designated). If you close the browser or exit your application prior to submitting, the application progress will be saved as a draft. You will be able to access and complete the application through “My Draft Applications” located on your Candidate Home page. Job Posting Title: Assistant Professor of Anatomy     Position Type: Faculty     Department: LSUAM VetMed - School of Comparative Biomedical Sciences (Alexander Murashov (00082114))     Work Location: Veterinary Medicine     Pay Grade: Academic     Job Description: The Anatomy Assistant Professor (Clinical track) participates in the delivery of lecture and laboratory material (gross- and micro-anatomy) for the Form & Function courses to first year veterinary students in the veterinary professional curriculum; assists with the preparation of all teaching materials related to the assigned teaching responsibilities; conducts scholarly activities; mentors professional and graduate students and contributes service at the department, school, and university levels. Job Responsibilities: 60% - The assistant professor participates in the delivery of lecture material and the laboratory anatomy courses to first year veterinary students in the veterinary professional curriculum. 25% -The assistant professor assists with the preparation of all teaching materials related to the assigned teaching responsibilities. These duties include doing prosections, embalming, and maintaining specimen collection, and performing other duties as assigned 10% -The assistant professor mentors professional and graduate students and contributes service at the department, school, and university level. 5% - The assistant professor conducts scholarly activities. Other duties as assigned. Minimum Requirements: DVM or equivalent (Applicants with a Ph.D. in a related field are encouraged to apply.) Preferred Qualifications: Ph.D. and 1+ years of teaching experience in gross anatomy laboratory/lecture settings Special and Physical Qualifications: Safety and Security Sensitive Job - Safety and security-sensitive positions are defined as those positions where any form of substance abuse may affect University activities through unsafe work behavior/performance or error in judgment; or where substance abuse could jeopardize the safety and well-being of employees, other personnel, or the general public, or cause significant damage to University property. This includes positions requiring firearms, access to controlled substances, handling hazardous material, operating heavy equipment or machinery, etc. These positions are subject to drug screens per PS-67/FASOP HR-04. FERPA - As with all LSU employees, due to this position title and/or responsibilities, this position has an obligation to assist students in the acquisition of necessary services. Should a student appear in need of or seek assistance with issues of an emotional, behavioral, or mental health related matter they should be referred to the proper service within the Student Health Center. In situations where the student creates an impression that they are a danger to themselves or others, the advisor should immediately contact the Care Team, the Dean of Students or the appropriate law enforcement agency. The employee outside of those rights contained within FERPA should create no impression of client or patient confidentiality. Requires standing, sitting, walking, bending, reaching (above shoulder), reaching (below shoulder), vision (near sight), vision (far sight), crawling, twisting, pushing/pulling, climbing, squatting, lifting more than 50 pounds, and lifting less than 50 pounds.     Additional Job Description: Special Instructions: Please attach ALL required documents under the \"Resume/CV\" section of your application. Please provide your CV/resume and three professional references including name, title, phone number and e-mail address. A copy of your transcript(s) may be attached to your application (if applicable). However, original transcripts are required prior to hire. For questions or concerns regarding the status of your application or salary ranges, please contact Dr. Alexander Murashov at amurashov@lsu.edu.     Skills Needed:     Preferred Skills:     Posting Date: July 31, 2026     Closing Date (Open Until Filled if No Date Specified): October 31, 2026    Additional Position Information: Background Check - An offer of employment is contingent on a satisfactory pre-employment background check. Benefits - LSU offers outstanding benefits to eligible employees and their dependents including health, life, dental, and vision insurance; flexible spending accounts; retirement options; various leave options; paid holidays; wellness benefits; tuition exemption for qualified positions; training and development opportunities; employee discounts; and more!     Positions approved to work outside the State of Louisiana shall be employed through Louisiana State University’s partner, nextSource Workforce Solutions, for Employer of Record Services including but not limited to employment, benefits, payroll, and tax compliance. Positions employed through Employer of Record Services will be offered benefits and retirement as applicable through their provider and will not be eligible for State of Louisiana benefits and retirement.     Essential Position (Y/N): Y     LSU is an Equal Opportunity Employer.     All candidates must have valid U.S. work authorization at the time of hire and maintain that valid work authorization throughout employment. Changes in laws, regulations, or government policies may impact the university’s ability to employ individuals in certain positions.     HCM Contact Information: For questions or concerns related to updating your application with attachments (e.g., resumes, RS:17 documents), date of birth, or reactivating applications, please contact the LSU Human Resources Management Office at 225-578-8200 or email HR@lsu.edu .  For questions or concerns regarding the status of your application or salary ranges, please contact the department using the information provided in the Special Instructions section of this job posting."
    },
    "https://lsu.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/LSU/job/Veterinary-Medicine/Assistant-or-Associate-Professor-of-Veterinary-Practice-Skills_R00117566": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Professor of Veterinary Practice Skills",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-07-31\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nFull time\n\nAll Job Postings will close at 12:01a.m. CST (1:01a.m. EST) on the specified Closing Date (if designated). If you close the browser or exit your application prior to submitting, the application progress will be saved as a draft. You will be able to access and complete the application through “My Draft Applications” located on your Candidate Home page. Job Posting Title: Assistant or Associate Professor of Veterinary Practice Skills     Position Type: Faculty     Department: LSUAM VetMed - School of Veterinary Clinical Sciences (Britta Sigrid Leise (00002280))     Work Location: Veterinary Medicine     Pay Grade: Academic     Job Description: The Department of Veterinary Clinical Sciences at Louisiana State University is searching for an Assistant or Associate Professor of Veterinary Practice Skills. This position is non-tenure track with starting level commensurate with experience. Based on their skill set, this person will primarily assist in a variety of teaching and examination activities including laboratory sessions in clinical skills, anesthesia/surgical skills, and communication skills, facilitation of small student groups in problem-based learning courses, and OSCE assessments. Expectation for more lead instructor roles and responsibilities within lab topics will be encouraged over time. Additionally, this employee will participate in outreach opportunities for LSU Vet Med relevant to these primary facets of their position. Opportunities for involvement in clinical education, classroom education, and research may be considered dependent on the need of the veterinary teaching hospital, pre-clinical curricular program, and available time in the employee's schedule that does not interfere with the primary responsibilities of this position. Job Responsibilities: 70%- Provide instruction in clinical and surgical skills laboratories across years 1-4 in the DVM program, including supervising veterinary students in pre-operative, operative, and post-operative care of live animals. 10%- Facilitate small-group sessions in communication skills labs and problem-based learning across years 1-3 in the DVM program. 10%- Participate in objective structured clinical skills examinations (OSCEs) as an examiner, animal handler, and/or assistant. 5%- Support the development and maintenance of relevant low fidelity models used in clinical and surgical skills labs. 4%- Other duties as assigned. 1%- Drive university vehicles to collect program donations and move program assets to/from outreach events. Minimum Qualifications: Assistant Professor : DVM or equivalent degree; three years of veterinary clinical practice experience. Associate Professor : DVM or equivalent degree; five years of academic experience in veterinary education AND veterinary clinical practice experience, with internal review by LSU for relevant qualifications. Minimum Certifications/Licenses : Ability to acquire a faculty or full veterinary license in the state of Louisiana. Preferred Qualifications: DVM or equivalent;  five or more years of academic experience in veterinary education AND veterinary clinical practice experience AND two or more years of adult education experience. Strong preference for experience in basic veterinary surgery. Preferred experience in any of the following aspects of veterinary education: basic veterinary surgical training, veterinary anesthesia, communication skills, clinical skills, small group learning, adult learning. Preferred Certifications/Licenses: Masters or other post-graduate degree in education   Special and Physical Qualifications: Vehicle Operation -University employees whose principal responsibilities of employment include operating a vehicle, performing maintenance on a vehicle, or supervising any public employee who operates or maintains a vehicle and positions with an expectation to travel will require an MVR and are subject to drug screens per PM-33/PS-67/FASOP HR-04. Safety and Security Sensitive Job - Safety and security-sensitive positions are defined as those positions where any form of substance abuse may affect University activities through unsafe work behavior/performance or error in judgment; or where substance abuse could jeopardize the safety and well-being of employees, other personnel, or the general public, or cause significant damage to University property. This includes positions requiring firearms, access to controlled substances, handling hazardous material, operating heavy equipment or machinery, etc. These positions are subject to drug screens per PS-67/FASOP HR-04. Minor Contact -This position and/or position's responsibilities require work directly with minors. Finger printing and National Sex Offender Registry check required per FASOP HR-04 FERPA -As with all LSU employees, due to this position title and/or responsibilities, this position has an obligation to assist students in the acquisition of necessary services. Should a student appear in need of or seek assistance with issues of an emotional, behavioral, or mental health related matter they should be referred to the proper service within the Student Health Center. In situations where the student creates an impression that they are a danger to themselves or others, the advisor should immediately contact the Care Team, the Dean of Students or the appropriate law enforcement agency. The employee outside of those rights contained within FERPA should create no impression of client or patient confidentiality.   Requires standing, sitting, walking, bending, reaching (above shoulder), reaching (below shoulder), vision (near sight), vision (far sight), crawling, twisting, pushing/pulling, climbing, squatting, lifting more than 50 pounds, and lifting less than 50 pounds.      Additional Job Description: Special Instructions: Please attach ALL required documents under the \"Resume/CV\" section of your application. Please provide your CV/resume and three professional references including name, title, phone number and e-mail address. A copy of your transcript(s) may be attached to your application (if applicable). However, original transcripts are required prior to hire. For questions or concerns regarding the status of your application or salary ranges, please contact Dr. Britta Leise at bleise@lsu.edu.     Skills Needed:     Preferred Skills:     Posting Date: July 31, 2026     Closing Date (Open Until Filled if No Date Specified): October 31, 2026    Additional Position Information: Background Check - An offer of employment is contingent on a satisfactory pre-employment background check. Benefits - LSU offers outstanding benefits to eligible employees and their dependents including health, life, dental, and vision insurance; flexible spending accounts; retirement options; various leave options; paid holidays; wellness benefits; tuition exemption for qualified positions; training and development opportunities; employee discounts; and more!     Positions approved to work outside the State of Louisiana shall be employed through Louisiana State University’s partner, nextSource Workforce Solutions, for Employer of Record Services including but not limited to employment, benefits, payroll, and tax compliance. Positions employed through Employer of Record Services will be offered benefits and retirement as applicable through their provider and will not be eligible for State of Louisiana benefits and retirement.     Essential Position (Y/N):     LSU is an Equal Opportunity Employer.     All candidates must have valid U.S. work authorization at the time of hire and maintain that valid work authorization throughout employment. Changes in laws, regulations, or government policies may impact the university’s ability to employ individuals in certain positions.     HCM Contact Information: For questions or concerns related to updating your application with attachments (e.g., resumes, RS:17 documents), date of birth, or reactivating applications, please contact the LSU Human Resources Management Office at 225-578-8200 or email HR@lsu.edu .  For questions or concerns regarding the status of your application or salary ranges, please contact the department using the information provided in the Special Instructions section of this job posting."
